lta.go:318 call site — comment corrected; oversize now falls through to FailedFiles.\n  8. **history.go:1548 scanSessionInventoryFileRecords** — 5th reader, treats \"exceeds maximum size\" as a refusal. LEFT: `graph neighbors --direction in` = **none** (dead code, as the #483 commit already recorded). Its comment governs nothing.\n  9. **transcript_cache_migration.go:76** — 6th reader (cache-retention sweep). LEFT DELIBERATELY: it compares the cached disk copy to the checkpoint copy to prove deletion is safe; an unreadable (oversize) cached copy means it cannot prove safety, so refusing is fail-safe. Making it per-file would be a behavior change in a destructive path, out of the finding's scope. Candidate follow-up: retain that one file instead of refusing the sweep.\n  10. memory_state.go:152 memoryErrorCode — code list. no change.\n  11. memory_abstract.go:937 — unrelated producer (abstract input bound); now explicitly pinned as STILL-refusing.\n  12. history_single_read_test.go:65 — test, unaffected.\n  \n  CHANGE (145+/11−, 3 files):\n  - internal/cli/history_delta.go:237-260 — `if errors.Is(err, errHistoryTranscriptTooLarge) { return false }` ahead of the code-string check; doc + call-site comments. Keyed on the sentinel, not the code string, so every OTHER bound breach still refuses and still leaves the prior overlay byte-identical.\n  - Delta's OWN partial-success notion threaded, nothing invented: the file joins `overlay.FailedFiles` + `stats.Failed` (durable identity in the overlay, surfaced by doctor/stats `failed_files`), `complete()` false so no surface claims coverage, overlay published for every other session. Verified nothing from the over-cap file can enter the overlay: file path returns on `file.Size` before open; store path's base.SafeReadAll returns nil data on breach.\n  - internal/cli/history_oversize_tolerance_test.go +90 — TestShortTermDeltaSkipsAnOversizeTranscriptAndIndexesTheRest (2 readable sessions indexed, huge one in FailedFiles, state current, !complete()); TestShortTermDeltaRefusesEveryBoundBreachExceptTheTranscriptCap (both directions of the classifier incl. store-classified refusal).\n  - internal/cli/history_delta_test.go — TestShortTermDeltaBoundFailurePreservesPriorOverlay RENAMED→TestShortTermDeltaOversizeTranscriptIsRecordedNotRefused, asserts the new contract. Its old general property survives in TestHistorySessionInventoryCeilingPreservesProjectionAndOverlay + TestShortTermDeltaMembershipRacePreservesOverlay (verified both exist and pass).\n  \n  PRIOR-DECISION CONFLICT (brain recall, cite): a recorded invariant (architecture.locking.invariant) lists \"making memoryErrInputTooLarge skippable into FailedFiles\" among five changes WITHDRAWN in an earlier session, on the grounds that TestShortTermDeltaBoundFailurePreservesPriorOverlay asserts the opposite. That rationale was deference to a test NAME, not a correctness argument; the name encoded the defect. Overruled by this finding + the no-partial-bytes proof. Recorded the reversal: `entire brain remember --path architecture.history.decision --branch main` (fact created 2026-10-25T04:40:30Z).\n  \n  VERIFICATION\n  1. Targeted, -v, names confirmed RUN+PASS: TestShortTermDeltaSkipsAnOversizeTranscriptAndIndexesTheRest, TestShortTermDeltaRefusesEveryBoundBreachExceptTheTranscriptCap, TestShortTermDeltaOversizeTranscriptIsRecordedNotRefused, TestHistoryIndexSkipsAnOversizeTranscriptAndIndexesTheRest, TestOversizeTranscriptIsSkippedByEveryInventoryReader, TestStoreSizeRefusalClassifiesAsTheSkippableOversizeError, TestOverridableProductionBoundsKeepTheirDefaults — all PASS. Broader `-run 'ShortTerm|Oversize|Delta|History'` ok 21.3s.\n  2. WEAKENINGS (each patch asserted unique-match in python before running; file restored from backup + sha verified c8f2bb1b after each):\n     A. delete the sentinel skip → RED x3 (both new tests + renamed test: \"refused the whole delta\").\n     B. delete the trailing memoryErrInputTooLarge clause → RED on the