{"parentUuid":"30dbd10f-4a26-4523-9608-ce0cf0fd6730","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgNM55bSKT6mWwpv8WLdh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01614Cq8pkWQ7FkMvJXrc37w","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"ab99f4dd9546f1b34","summary":"Return a verified coverage gap on 483","message":"Good work, and the overrule of the recorded withdrawal is accepted — but I verified your \"the old general property survives\" claim and it does not hold. One gap to close.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED. You said the renamed test's general property survives in TestHistorySessionInventoryCeilingPreservesProjectionAndOverlay and TestShortTermDeltaMembershipRacePreservesOverlay. I read both. They do assert `bytes.Equal(after, before)` on the published overlay — but for DIFFERENT error classes: errHistorySessionInventoryDegraded and memoryErrSourceStale. Neither involves a bound breach.\n\nI then searched every overlay-preservation assertion in history_input_test.go, history_delta_test.go and history_oversize_tolerance_test.go. The `bytes.Equal(after, before)` guards cover symlink rejection, leaf swap, inventory ceiling and membership race. NONE covers memoryErrInputTooLarge. And TestShortTermDeltaOversizeTranscriptIsRecordedNotRefused now asserts `bytes.Equal` is FALSE — the opposite contract, correctly, for the transcript cap.\n\nSo: the behavioural guarantee \"a BOUND-BREACH refusal leaves the prior overlay byte-identical\" is now untested. TestShortTermDeltaRefusesEveryBoundBreachExceptTheTranscriptCap does not restore it — it asserts the classifier's boolean, not that the build declines to publish. That is a classifier standing in for the behaviour its predecessor asserted, and the recorded invariant you overruled was specifically about not letting a guarantee lapse in this subsystem. Your narrowing of the BEHAVIOUR is right; letting the guard for the remaining case lapse is the part to fix.\n\nWHAT I WANT: one behavioural test that a NON-transcript bound breach still leaves the published overlay byte-identical — the same shape as the ceiling test (read overlay bytes, run the build under withBrainWriteLock, assert it errored AND the bytes are unchanged), but reaching the build through a real bound breach rather than a hand-built error. Prefer lowering a real non-transcript bound on the delta's read path over injecting an error; if no non-transcript bound is actually reachable from that path, say so explicitly with the evidence and use the narrowest available seam, naming it as a seam in the test comment.\n\nThen prove it by weakening in the way that matters: make that refusal publish anyway (or return nil instead of the error) and confirm you see `--- FAIL: <your test>`. A `[build failed]` is not a red. Re-run prepush, same log discipline as before, and push to the same branch.\n\nTwo process notes, both credit to you: catching that weakening D matched twice and that the green run which followed proved nothing is exactly right — that failure mode has bitten me too. And keep GOMAXPROCS=4; load here is ~7 with other agents running.","type":"message","recipient":"ab99f4dd9546f1b34","recipient_kind":"agent","content":"Good work, and the overrule of the recorded withd…"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2551,"cache_read_input_tokens":291137,"output_tokens":2236,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1026},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2551,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2236,"cache_read_input_tokens":291137,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2551,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2551},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01614Cq8pkWQ7FkMvJXrc37w":{"to":"ab99f4dd9546f1b34","summary":"Return a verified coverage gap on 483","message":"Good work, and the overrule of the recorded withdrawal is accepted — but I verified your \"the old general property survives\" claim and it does not hold. One gap to close.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED. You said the renamed test's general property survives in TestHistorySessionInventoryCeilingPreservesProjectionAndOverlay and TestShortTermDeltaMembershipRacePreservesOverlay. I read both. They do assert `bytes.Equal(after, before)` on the published overlay — but for DIFFERENT error classes: errHistorySessionInventoryDegraded and memoryErrSourceStale. Neither involves a bound breach.\n\nI then searched every overlay-preservation assertion in history_input_test.go, history_delta_test.go and history_oversize_tolerance_test.go. The `bytes.Equal(after, before)` guards cover symlink rejection, leaf swap, inventory ceiling and membership race. NONE covers memoryErrInputTooLarge. And TestShortTermDeltaOversizeTranscriptIsRecordedNotRefused now asserts `bytes.Equal` is FALSE — the opposite contract, correctly, for the transcript cap.\n\nSo: the behavioural guarantee \"a BOUND-BREACH refusal leaves the prior overlay byte-identical\" is now untested. TestShortTermDeltaRefusesEveryBoundBreachExceptTheTranscriptCap does not restore it — it asserts the classifier's boolean, not that the build declines to publish. That is a classifier standing in for the behaviour its predecessor asserted, and the recorded invariant you overruled was specifically about not letting a guarantee lapse in this subsystem. Your narrowing of the BEHAVIOUR is right; letting the guard for the remaining case lapse is the part to fix.\n\nWHAT I WANT: one behavioural test that a NON-transcript bound breach still leaves the published overlay byte-identical — the same shape as the ceiling test (read overlay bytes, run the build under withBrainWriteLock, assert it errored AND the bytes are unchanged), but reaching the build through a real bound breach rather than a hand-built error. Prefer lowering a real non-transcript bound on the delta's read path over injecting an error; if no non-transcript bound is actually reachable from that path, say so explicitly with the evidence and use the narrowest available seam, naming it as a seam in the test comment.\n\nThen prove it by weakening in the way that matters: make that refusal publish anyway (or return nil instead of the error) and confirm you see `--- FAIL: <your test>`. A `[build failed]` is not a red. Re-run prepush, same log discipline as before, and push to the same branch.\n\nTwo process notes, both credit to you: catching that weakening D matched twice and that the green run which followed proved nothing is exactly right — that failure mode has bitten me too. And keep GOMAXPROCS=4; load here is ~7 with other agents running."}},"serverClassifierRequest":"984db351-8474-422d-96af-db1570cd348a","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CgNM55FMbx8YXQUrKorUe","requestRef":"669b3b96-70a1-4c47-aaa1-f5a7dab9c9de","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5[1m]","type":"assistant","uuid":"02928d8e-0c0f-4081-a1bb-7742de00affc","timestamp":"2026-10-25T04:46:04.178Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"80327498-7bd2-42a4-985a-9acba8ccaf85","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-history-oversize","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","version":"2.1.310","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floofy-discovering-bachman"}
