{"parentUuid":"502ff5b7-e879-4ff9-8188-884e498536bb","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgNNaNsgmWiXRxJhdSotQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Correcting myself: **no agent has pushed yet.** `db2d4ff9` was my own earlier #489 commit — I read the branch head, saw a SHA I didn't recognise from this turn, and reported it as the agent's work without checking whether it was new. `git log fix/...@{upstream}` showed zero new commits, which I'd actually already run. That's the kind of claim I should verify before stating, not after.\n\nReading it was still worth it on two counts:\n\n**It confirms the finding against real code.** The object probe's error path is literally `if err != nil { unavailable = append(...); break }` — every failure, transient or not, classified as \"the object is gone.\"\n\n**It surfaced a second issue my refactor introduced.** The unreadable-chunk case used to wrap with `%w`; I changed it to `%v` into a `[]string`, and the all-or-nothing wrapper now rebuilds a bare `errors.New`. Message preserved, identity gone. That matters precisely *for this finding*: if the probe fails because the context was cancelled, the old code let an upstream `errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)` match. Flattened to a string, cancellation is indistinguishable from anything else upstream — the same conflation being removed one layer down. Fixing the classification while leaving identity flattened would just move the problem.\n\nSent to that agent with a one-character weakening to prove it (`%w` → `%v`).\n\nSo the honest state: three agents running, **nothing pushed by any of them**, and the only things on the remote are my own commits on #484, #483 and #477."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1386,"cache_read_input_tokens":312289,"output_tokens":510,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server