{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","hash":"3b2171b502c58107867c5a714a2acde11f7a41f6d35e19dd7d346257f34ce5d2","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UzmTpXofuP4rrHqVreWeLY","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"merge-graph-fixes-and-improve-agent-adoption","summary":"All four findings landed; one design call found","message":"All four findings are fixed, pushed and green — plus the skeptic pass you asked for on #489, which found one thing you should carry into the handoff as a DESIGN CALL, not a defect.\n\nFINAL STATE, all green, all conflict-free, Approvals the only blocker:\n  #477 feat/mcp-surface-reachable           17f6bdc3\n  #483 fix/history-index-tolerates-oversize  201fe6eb\n  #484 feat/brief-ancestor-staleness         8d3f4ce8\n  #489 fix/export-skips-vanished-checkpoints ce563535\n\n#489 SKEPTIC PASS. The fix classifies \"object missing\" by `cat-file -e` EXIT CODE rather than stderr prose. I reproduced the codes on real git 2.54.0 myself rather than trusting the report: present=0 silent, resolved-but-absent=1 silent, unresolvable=128 with fatal:. Sound, and 9 weakenings were red. It also adopted the error-identity point I sent — `unavailable` is now []error wrapping with %w, so errors.Is reaches both errGitObjectMissing and context.Canceled.\n\nTHE DESIGN CALL. I then tested the one case the agent disclosed it could not: a promisor partial clone. The probe runs through runCheckpointGitLocal, which sets GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1. Measured: a blob that is absent locally but FETCHABLE from the promisor remote also exits 1 silent — byte-identical to a pruned object that is gone for good. (With lazy fetch allowed it exits 0, because it fetches.) So under no-egress on a partial clone, \"absent\" is genuinely ambiguous and the code cannot tell the two apart without egress. #489 resolves it toward \"gone\" and skips the session, and `unavailableSessionsAreFatal` refuses only when usable==0 — there is no PARTIAL-shrink guard. A previously larger published corpus can therefore be replaced by a smaller one with only a warning.\n\nThat configuration is live, not hypothetical: cli's brain was frozen earlier in this session precisely because transcript and metadata blobs were promisor-absent, and I fixed it by fetching 422 of them by hand. It is also the same promisor shape as your #372.\n\nI deliberately did NOT patch it, because both obvious fixes are wrong:\n  - \"a promisor remote means absent might be fetchable, so refuse\" re-breaks #489 exactly where it was needed — cli IS a promisor clone and its squash-destroyed commits are unfetchable anywhere.\n  - \"refuse if the new corpus is smaller\" breaks `privacy exclude`, which legitimately shrinks via tombstones — which is what #488 does.\nA correct guard has to be tombstone-aware, so it is a design decision and it is on Suhaan's list, recorded in brain at architecture.export.constraint. #489 is still a strict improvement on what it replaced (which refused everything, permanently, and preserved nothing), so I would not hold it for this.\n\nALSO WORTH YOUR D2: both agents independently hit a pre-existing landmine. `internal/cli` runs 232s idle but 805-855s under contention, against go test's 10m DEFAULT, which test:prepush never raises (CI uses -timeout 20m in nightly/release). Under parallel load the gate fails on correct code and the panic names whichever test was running at the alarm. If your fresh-env runs touch this package, use GOFLAGS=-timeout=30m rather than trusting a red.\n\nBrain is FINAL as of now. Go ahead with D2. Sequential-merge sim pass 1 is done on my side: all 13 merge clean in order and the combined tree compiles with 0 vet findings; the suite run on the combined tree is the remaining half and I am running it now that the agents have freed the CPU."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
